// The finite supported matrix: the databases the engine is qualified against,
// the runtime that serves each one, and how this checkout reaches it.
//
// One table, shared by the atomicity suite (tests/card/actions/atomic-saves)
// and the qualification suite (tests/deploy/runtime-db-matrix), so "a
// supported backend" means one thing in both places.
//
// Each entry names the runtime that serves the database and how the database
// is reached from this checkout:
//
//   id         runtime   database                     reached by
//   ---------  --------  ---------------------------  --------------------------
//   sqlite     node      SQLite                       better-sqlite3, scratch file
//   postgres   node      PostgreSQL                   PGlite: the real engine
//   mysql      node      MySQL 8.4                    the vendor server binaries
//   d1         workerd   Cloudflare D1                miniflare's local D1 binding
//   libsql     node      libSQL (Turso)               the Hrana v2 mock server
//   bunny      node      Bunny Database               the Hrana server, Bunny's driver
//
// The emulated rows (D1 under miniflare, libSQL and Bunny over the mock Hrana
// server) are not the hosted services. Hosted D1, Turso and Bunny Database are
// reached by tools/hosted-conformance/run.mjs, which needs credentials this
// checkout does not carry, and native MySQL needs the vendor binaries, which
// tools/mysql/fetch-mysql.mjs downloads. A cell whose prerequisite is absent is
// a skip with its reason attached, never a pass.
//
// The engine speaks one dialect (SQLite) to every adapter (src/server/
// driver.ts), so the matrix varies the database, not the SQL. The MySQL
// adapter translates that dialect at the edge; the PostgreSQL adapter
// translates it too.

